design choices and why (folded from the brief, made concrete against
the actual ir.ML/MCP_Tools.thy code)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

- fresh state, not a repl: building via Theory.begin_theory /
  Toplevel.make_state exactly mirrors Ir.init's from_specs (single
  spec case) but is written independently in MCP_Profile_Time.thy --
  ir.ML is verbatim-reused (MIT header) and shared with two other
  in-flight worktrees; editing it is out of scope and would collide.
  The state this function builds is never inserted into Ir's
  repl_tab, so nothing here can be seen or mutated by repl_* tools.
- per-transition timing that survives a failure: Timing.timing itself
  does NOT catch exceptions (Pure/General/timing.ML:69-73 -- `f x` is
  called unguarded, `result start` only runs after `f x` returns), so
  timing a call that may raise means wrapping it first:
  `Timing.timing (Exn.result f) x`. This is exactly the idiom
  Timing.command_timing (timing.ML:108-121) itself uses. Confirmed by
  reading Pure/General/timing.ML directly (the flatpak-bundled
  source at .../de.tum.in.isabelle.Isabelle/.../src/Pure/General/timing.ML)
  -- this refines the brief's suggested one-liner
  `Timing.timing (Toplevel.command_exception false tr) st`, which
  would lose the timing of a failing transition entirely (the
  exception propagates out of Timing.timing before `result start`
  ever runs).
- failure policy: PARTIAL RESULTS (see spec decision for the full
  argument) -- the fold stops at the first failing transition, that
  transition's own (measured) timing and error message are included
  in the report, and nothing after it is attempted (there is no valid
  "after": the fold's state never advanced past the failure, so
  further transitions would run against the wrong state and their
  timing would misattribute work to code that never executed against
  the intended prior state).
- sort key: raw `#elapsed (#timing entry)` (a Time.time), NOT a
  string-parsed Timing.message -- sorting happens on the typed record
  before formatting, so there is no reliance on Timing.message's
  textual shape for anything but the final human-readable line.
- registered under session MCP-HOL (not MCP-Tools): the tool needs
  Toplevel/Outer_Syntax/Thy_Info against real HOL theories, so it is a
  HOL-anchored theory like MCP_Repl.thy, importing "MCP-HOL.MCP" (the
  footgun-avoiding entry point documented in MCP.thy) rather than
  "MCP-Tools.MCP_Tools" directly.

assumptions and how to test them
-----------------------------------

T1  the report lists exactly the right number of commands for a
    proof text with no errors, and the total-elapsed summary line's
    count matches.
    test [ml unit]: profile a short multi-command lemma+by proof;
    assert the report contains N per-command lines and a total line
    naming N.
T2  slowest-elapsed-first sorting: a deliberately expensive command
    (a large arithmetic/simp goal, or an explicit induction over a
    sizeable recursive definition) sorts above a trivial one
    (e.g. a bare `lemma "True" by simp`), regardless of which one
    appears first in the input text.
    test [ml unit]: text with a cheap command first and an expensive
    one second; assert the expensive command's line appears before
    the cheap one's in the report.
T3  a proof containing an error is handled per the documented
    PARTIAL RESULTS policy: the report includes every command up to
    and including the failing one (marked), the failing command's own
    timing is present (not silently dropped), and the report contains
    no line for any command that would have come after it.
    test [ml unit]: text = [ok command, failing command
    ("apply nonexistent_method" or similar), ok command that would
    never run]; assert report has exactly 2 entries, the second
    marked as an error and naming the failure, and the third
    command's text/name does not appear anywhere in the report.
T4  mcp_tool wiring: the tool is reachable via MCP_Tool.run under its
    theory-qualified name with the {theory, text} argument shape, and
    rejects a spurious argument key (registry-level validate, not
    hand-rolled).
    test [ml unit]: MCP_Tool.run \<^context>
    "MCP_Profile_Time.profile_proof_time" [("theory", main),
    ("text", "lemma \"True\" by simp")] succeeds and contains a
    per-command line; a spurious key errors naming the key.
T5  read-only / no repl mutation: calling the tool does not create,
    modify, or leak any entry into Ir's repl_tab.
    test [ml unit]: call Ir.repls () (or the MCP_Repl "repls"
    dispatch) before and after a profile_proof_time call over a repl
    id that happens to share a name with nothing yet created; assert
    the listing is unchanged (in particular, no REPL exists under any
    name the profiling call touched internally).

