open questions / follow-ups (not blocking this experiment)
------------------------------------------------------------
- no timeout on the whole run: unlike Ir.exec_text (default_timeout
  10s), this tool runs the WHOLE text unbounded -- appropriate for a
  profiler (a slow command is exactly what it exists to find; a
  timeout would just turn "the slow command" into "an error" without
  telling the agent which command was slow, or with what magnitude).
  Revisit if profiling ever needs a hard wall-clock budget for a
  runaway sledgehammer call.
- GC time only distinguishes GC-bound commands if is_relevant_time's
  1ms floor is exceeded; sub-millisecond commands report as zero
  across the board. Not worth chasing at this MVP stage --
  the point is finding the multi-second outlier, not micro-profiling.

addendum: profile_proof_time_grouped (hierarchical, decided 2026-08-13)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

spec: D-2026-08-13-proof-profiler-time-hierarchical. status: landed,
same PR/branch as the base tool.

advertised metadata: name profile_proof_time_grouped, same
inputSchema ({theory, text}, both quoted param names for the same
outer-syntax-keyword-collision reason as the base tool) and
annotations (read_only) as profile_proof_time.

mechanism: `MCP_Profile_Time.collect` (extracted from the base tool's
`profile` so both tools share one code path for building the scratch
state and folding transitions) now tags every `entry` with `level =
Toplevel.level` of the post-transition state (or the pre-transition
state, unchanged, for a failed transition -- it never advances).
`group_entries` folds the document-order entry list into blocks: a
block closes on the entry whose level reads 0 and a new one opens
right after; Toplevel.level is 0 at theory level and >0 anywhere
inside a goal/proof, so this is exactly "one lemma/theorem statement
through its close", or a singleton block for a command that never
leaves level 0 (definition, a fun whose termination proof is internal
to that one transition). No hardcoded command-name whitelist --
tried and rejected first, because it would silently miss any
goal-opening command the list didn't happen to name (interpretation,
lift_definition with a proof obligation, ...).
format_grouped_report sorts blocks by their own summed elapsed time,
slowest first; within a block, entries are still sorted
slowest-command-first (reusing the base tool's `format_entry`/
`by_elapsed_desc`, indented one level).

